.TH HD-BACKUP-PREP 8 "October 2026" "hd-backup" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-backup-prep \- turn a drive into a backup carrier for hd-backup
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hd-backup-prep
.RB [ \-\-owner
.IR user ]
.I device pool
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hd-backup-prep
wipes the whole drive
.I device
and creates the ZFS pool
.I pool
on it, as a carrier for the backups of
.BR hd\-backup (1).
Everything on the drive is lost.
It shows the drive, its size, the pool and the mountpoint, and asks before it
changes anything.
Run it as root.
.PP
It removes the ZFS labels, the signatures and the partition table of the
drive, zeroes its first 100 MiB and has the kernel and udev read it again.
Then it creates the pool with:
.IP \(bu 2
native encryption,
.BR aes\-256\-gcm ,
with a passphrase that
.BR zpool (8)
asks for;
.IP \(bu 2
.B zstd
compression and a record size of 4 MiB, to match the tarballs of
.BR hd\-backup ;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR ashift=12 ,
.B xattr=sa
and
.BR dnodesize=auto ;
.IP \(bu 2
the mountpoint
.IR /mnt/pool .
.PP
The dataset
.I pool/reserved
reserves a tenth of the drive, to keep part of it free.
The mountpoint is given to the user who ran
.BR sudo ,
or to
.IR user ,
so that user can write the backups.
.PP
Use the name of the pool as the label of the carrier in
.BR hd\-backup .
Afterwards,
.BR hd\-backup\-load (8)
and
.BR hd\-backup\-unload (8)
connect and disconnect the drive.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-owner " user"
The user who gets the mountpoint, instead of the user who ran
.BR sudo ,
or root without it.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help and exit.
.SH EXAMPLE
.RS
.nf
sudo hd-backup-prep /dev/disk/by-id/ata-WDC_WD40EFRX-68N32N0_WD-1234 bk01
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the pool is created, 1 on an error or when the question is answered
with no, 130 when it is interrupted.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd\-backup (1),
.BR hd\-backup\-load (8),
.BR hd\-backup\-unload (8),
.BR zpool\-create (8),
.BR zfsprops (7)
.SH AUTHOR
Erik Van Hamme <erik.vanhamme@gmail.com>
